# Git Commit Identity

Never commit with an inherited identity. Machine defaults have leaked into this
repository's history before (`root@EULT-PF3TPC11.adv.advantest.com`,
`philipp@fedora.fritz.box`, `Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>`), and so has
the work address on private commits.

## Decide from the remote, before the first commit

```bash
git remote -v            # which host and account owns this repository?
git config user.name     # what would the next commit be attributed to?
```

| Remote | `user.name` | `user.email` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `github.com/Philipp0205/...` and other personal remotes | `Philipp0205` | `36603238+Philipp0205@users.noreply.github.com` |
| `*.advantest.com` (e.g. `socgit.advantest.com`) or the `philipp-kurrle_ADVNTST` GitHub account | `philipp-kurrle_ADVNTST` | `philipp.kurrle@advantest.com` |

## Set it per repository, never globally

A global identity is what causes the wrong account to sign the next clone.

```bash
# ❌ BAD - leaks into every other repository
git config --global user.email philipp.kurrle@advantest.com

# ✅ GOOD - private project, scoped to this clone
git config user.name Philipp0205
git config user.email 36603238+Philipp0205@users.noreply.github.com

# ✅ GOOD - Advantest work project, scoped to this clone
git config user.name philipp-kurrle_ADVNTST
git config user.email philipp.kurrle@advantest.com
```

Verify with `git log -1 --format='%an <%ae>'` after committing.

Do not rewrite existing commits to correct their author unless explicitly asked;
fix the config and move on.
